\frametitle{Four lines, on your own embeddings}
\begin{lstlisting}
import numpy as np
from ripser import ripser
from persim import plot_diagrams

X = model.encode(texts)                # or any embedding / hidden state
dgms = ripser(X, maxdim=1, thresh=2.0)["dgms"]
plot_diagrams(dgms, show=True)

h1 = dgms[1]
order = np.argsort(-(h1[:, 1] - h1[:, 0]))
print(h1[order][:5])                    # five longest-lived loops
        \end{lstlisting}
        \vspace{0.4em}
        \small
        \alert{Run this before the lecture and show saved figures} --- Rips on real data is
        not a live-demo activity. Passing \texttt{distance\_matrix=True} lets you use cosine
        or any learned metric instead of Euclidean.

        \vspace{0.3em}
        Software: \texttt{ripser} (fastest for Rips), \texttt{GUDHI} (broadest),
        \texttt{giotto-tda} (scikit-learn \texttt{fit}/\texttt{transform} API --- drops into
        an existing pipeline directly), \texttt{CubicalRipser} (images and feature maps).
